<!-- BLUEPRINT -->
<section id="blueprint"><div class="wrap">
  <div class="section-head">
    <span class="eyebrow">The blueprint · jgen</span>
    <h2>From one XML file to a running workflow service</h2>
    <p class="lead">Finito ships an inbuilt blueprint — <code>bp-wfcustom</code> — that <b>jgen</b> expands into a complete Chenile service: an <code>-api</code> module and a <code>-service</code> module, wired to your state machine.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="pipe" style="margin-bottom:36px">
    <div class="step"><div class="ic">📄</div><h4>your states.xml</h4><p>+ a few answers</p></div>
    <div class="conn">→</div>
    <div class="step out"><div class="ic">🧬</div><h4>jgen · bp-wfcustom</h4><p>the inbuilt blueprint</p></div>
    <div class="conn">→</div>
    <div class="fork">
      <div class="step"><div class="ic">📘</div><h4>returns-api</h4><p>StateEntity + service contract</p></div>
      <div class="step"><div class="ic">⚙️</div><h4>returns-service</h4><p>actions, config, tests</p></div>
      <div class="step mermaid-out"><div class="ic">🖼️</div><h4>diagrams</h4><p>Mermaid + PlantUML</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="grid-2">
    <div>
      <h3 style="margin-bottom:12px">The contract is standardized</h3>
      <p style="color:var(--slate)">Your entity extends <code>AbstractStateEntity</code>; the generated API uses the shared <code>StateEntityService&lt;T&gt;</code> — so every workflow service exposes the same verbs:</p>
      <div class="codewrap">
        <div class="bar"><i class="dotr"></i> StateEntityService&lt;Returns&gt;</div>
<pre><code><span class="tok-kw">process</span>(entity, event, payload)
<span class="tok-kw">processById</span>(id, event, payload)
<span class="tok-kw">create</span>(entity)
<span class="tok-kw">retrieve</span>(id)
<span class="tok-kw">getAllowedActionsAndMetadata</span>(id)</code></pre>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3 style="margin-bottom:12px">Toggle features at generation time</h3>
      <p style="color:var(--slate)">The blueprint asks what you need and wires it in — no boilerplate to hand-write:</p>
      <div class="grid-2" style="gap:12px">
        <div class="card" style="padding:16px"><b>JPA</b><p style="margin:4px 0 0;font-size:.86rem;color:var(--muted)">persistence for your entity</p></div>
        <div class="card" style="padding:16px"><b>Security</b><p style="margin:4px 0 0;font-size:.86rem;color:var(--muted)">ACL perms on events</p></div>
        <div class="card" style="padding:16px"><b>Activities</b><p style="margin:4px 0 0;font-size:.86rem;color:var(--muted)">mandatory/optional checklists</p></div>
        <div class="card" style="padding:16px"><b>Enablement</b><p style="margin:4px 0 0;font-size:.86rem;color:var(--muted)">toggle transitions by config</p></div>
        <div class="card" style="padding:16px"><b>Cloud switch</b><p style="margin:4px 0 0;font-size:.86rem;color:var(--muted)">edge ↔ cloud routing</p></div>
        <div class="card" style="padding:16px"><b>BDD tests</b><p style="margin:4px 0 0;font-size:.86rem;color:var(--muted)">generated Cucumber specs</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div></section>

<!-- CLI -->
<section class="bg-soft" id="cli"><div class="wrap">
  <div class="section-head">
    <span class="eyebrow">stm-cli</span>
    <h2>A command line for your state machines</h2>
    <p class="lead"><code>stm-cli</code> works straight off your XML — validate a flow, generate its diagram, and even scaffold test cases from the transitions.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="codewrap" style="max-width:720px;margin:0 auto 26px">
    <div class="bar"><i class="dotr"></i> terminal</div>
<pre><code><span class="tok-cm"># render a Mermaid state diagram from a flow</span>
stm-cli --mermaid returns-states.xml

<span class="tok-cm"># generate test cases that walk every transition</span>
stm-cli --generate-test-cases returns-states.xml

<span class="tok-cm"># render a state diagram for each generated test path</span>
stm-cli --render-tests-as-state returns-states.xml</code></pre>
  </div>
  <div class="cli-opts">
    <div class="cli-opt"><code>-m, --mermaid</code><p>Emit a Mermaid-compatible state diagram for the flow.</p></div>
    <div class="cli-opt"><code>-t, --generate-test-cases</code><p>Generate test cases that exercise the machine's transitions.</p></div>
    <div class="cli-opt"><code>-r, --render-tests-as-state</code><p>Draw a state diagram for every generated test case path.</p></div>
  </div>
</div></section>

<!-- CHENILE GROUP OR STANDALONE -->
<section id="standalone"><div class="wrap">
  <div class="section-head">
    <span class="eyebrow">Where it fits</span>
    <h2>Part of Chenile — happy on its own</h2>
    <p class="lead">Finito is the state-machine core of the Chenile framework, but the engine has no framework strings attached. Use whichever door suits you.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="homes">
    <div class="home solo">
      <h3>🧩 Standalone engine</h3>
      <p style="color:var(--slate)">Drop <code>org.chenile.stm</code> into any Java app. Read a flow with <code>XmlFlowReader</code> (or the <code>FluentFlowReader</code> API), then drive entities with <code>STM.proceed(...)</code>.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Plain Java — no Spring, no container required</li>
        <li>Pluggable strategies (scripting, security, store) with sensible no-op defaults</li>
        <li>Generate Mermaid / PlantUML from your XML with the CLI</li>
        <li>Perfect for embedding a workflow inside an existing service</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="home grp">
      <h3>🟠 Within the Chenile group</h3>
      <p style="color:var(--slate)">Use the full <b>workflow blueprint</b> and get a first-class Chenile service: persistence, security, activities, BDD tests and REST endpoints around your machine.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Generated <code>-api</code> + <code>-service</code> via <code>jgen bp-wfcustom</code></li>
        <li>Shares Chenile's interceptors, registry and messaging</li>
        <li>The same state machine, now a deployable microservice</li>
        <li>Versioned with the Chenile release train</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="callout amber" style="margin-top:26px">
    <div class="t">In one line</div>
    Finito belongs to the Chenile group and benefits from all of it — but the state machine at its heart is a small, dependency-light Java library you can use entirely on its own.
  </div>
</div></section>

